00:31:18,500 --> 00:31:23,560
There is prophecy in it, just like there's a fish in it, but that's not the primary part of the story.

168
00:31:23,560 --> 00:31:26,220
It's not about him delivering the prophecies that he delivered.

169
00:31:26,220 --> 00:31:31,000
It's a story about the prophet and his failure to deliver, as he should.

170
00:31:33,660 --> 00:31:38,800
And to be clear then, what is a prophet in the Old Testament and the Bible at large?

171
00:31:38,800 --> 00:31:42,080
A prophet is, a prophecy isn't something that tells the future, okay?

172
00:31:42,800 --> 00:31:45,180
A prophet is somebody who speaks the word of God.

173
00:31:45,180 --> 00:31:47,520
A priest would speak to God for the people.

174
00:31:47,520 --> 00:31:51,120
A prophet would speak to the people for God, and frequently to the king for God.

175
00:31:51,120 --> 00:31:55,780
And frequently, those messages would have some sort of ultimated in it.

176
00:31:55,780 --> 00:32:02,200
And it told the future in the same way that your mom tells the future when she says, if you don't clean your room, you're going to be grounded for the rest of your life, right?

177
00:32:02,780 --> 00:32:06,360
It's really easy to tell the future when you control the future, right?

178
00:32:06,980 --> 00:32:20,080
And so there would be these, the prophets would frequently come with promised punishment or discipline if the disobedience continued, or promises of faithfulness to a thousand generations, right?

179
00:32:20,080 --> 00:32:23,420
If, in fact, you would be faithful and cease from your disobedience.

180
00:32:23,420 --> 00:32:28,140
So in that sense, prophets would tell the future, but they were there to deliver messages from God.

181
00:32:28,140 --> 00:32:33,360
And their role was that they frequently delivered those messages to the king.

182
00:32:34,080 --> 00:32:37,180
And they often said, who is God saying should be king?

183
00:32:37,180 --> 00:32:38,580
So they were the king makers.

184
00:32:38,580 --> 00:32:40,620
It was an official role.

185
00:32:40,620 --> 00:32:43,580
It was a feared role, and the kings would fear the prophets.

186
00:32:43,580 --> 00:32:51,020
Before they would engage in international relations, they would check with the prophet as to, is this advisable and allowable?

187
00:32:51,020 --> 00:32:54,260
Before they entered war, the prophet would have to sanction it.

188
00:32:54,260 --> 00:33:02,160
So he was, he had a role more like secretary of state or something than like, you know, a guy walking around telling fortunes.

189
00:33:02,160 --> 00:33:03,780
You know, he's not a crystal ball guy.

190
00:33:03,780 --> 00:33:05,340
He's a secretary of state guy, okay?

191
00:33:05,340 --> 00:33:14,140
This particular prophet, Jonah, I would like to position in your mind who he is.

192
00:33:14,140 --> 00:33:19,400
And to do that, I'm going to need to step back in time a little bit, and I crave your indulgence, a very quick bit of history.

193
00:33:19,400 --> 00:33:27,020
Six months ago, when I was standing up here, we talked about a time in Exodus 34 when Moses stood on Mount Sinai and God revealed his character to Moses.

194
00:33:27,560 --> 00:33:41,600
He said, Yahweh, Yahweh, I'm a God merciful and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in steadfast love and faithfulness, keeping steadfast love for thousands of generations, forgiving iniquity and transgressions and sin, but who will by no means clear the guilty.

195
00:33:43,460 --> 00:33:56,660
He said that to Moses in response to the fact that having just told the people of Israel who gathered at the foot of Mount Sinai how they should live their lives around him, how they should worship him as a God who would be their chieftain and leader and live with him.

196
00:33:57,200 --> 00:33:58,240
He was not an idol.

197
00:33:58,240 --> 00:33:59,740
He was a single God.

198
00:33:59,740 --> 00:34:00,940
The Lord your God is one.

199
00:34:00,940 --> 00:34:05,080
And he told them all these things about how to live their entire nation, should live their life around him.

200
00:34:05,080 --> 00:34:13,500
They immediately chose instead to build a golden calf and say, we're going to worship you as if you were an idol, which is the way we understood God's working back in Egypt.

201
00:34:13,980 --> 00:34:19,600
And what we're going to do is we're going to, as if you were a beast that we could harness to drag us where we want to go.

202
00:34:19,600 --> 00:34:24,700
We want to treat you like someone we can propitiate to and manipulate you to give us what we want, right?

203
00:34:24,700 --> 00:34:27,020
And we're going to have a different relationship with you.

204
00:34:27,020 --> 00:34:33,640
We're going to have different festivals and different priests and different kinds of sacrifice so that we can live life in the way that we feel in control of.

205
00:34:35,020 --> 00:34:42,780
There was a reckoning that came from that, and Israel struggled with this transition to worshiping God in the way he directed them and understanding who he was.

206
00:34:42,780 --> 00:34:56,660
But hundreds of years later, finally, King David was born and brought the nation together, brought all the tribes together into a nation that did, in fact, live their lives around the worship of Yahweh as directed to Moses on Mount Sinai, right?

207
00:34:56,660 --> 00:35:01,660
Aaron had said, behold, Aaron was Moses' brother back when the golden calf.

208
00:35:01,800 --> 00:35:05,240
He had declared, behold, these are your gods who brought you out of Egypt.

209
00:35:05,240 --> 00:35:16,560
And there was a struggle against, when he talked about the golden calf, there was a struggle understanding who God really was until David was at last finally able to unify the country around this.

210
00:35:16,560 --> 00:35:24,860
And he made, with his faith in God, he built the nations to great, very large borders, and they were a dominant, influential force in the region.

211
00:35:25,360 --> 00:35:39,800
He passed this powerful country that was centered around God onto his son with the instruction that he should build a temple in the center of their major city so that the country could, in fact, live their lives around God as he instructed Moses on Mount Sinai in Exodus 34.

212
00:35:39,800 --> 00:35:42,240
And Solomon did that.

213
00:35:43,120 --> 00:35:56,140
There's this really excellent moment in 2 Chronicles 6 and 1 Kings 8 where Solomon is dedicating the temple to God.

214
00:35:56,140 --> 00:35:59,460
And he prays a prayer that really you should read over and over.

215
00:35:59,460 --> 00:36:00,740
It's a fantastic prayer.

216
00:36:00,740 --> 00:36:02,480
It's recorded in the Bible multiple times.

217
00:36:03,640 --> 00:36:17,160
But a key part of it, he says, Lord God, when we sin and when we do receive the punishment that you told us about on Mount Sinai, when you don't let evil go unpunished, even though you're gracious to thousands of generations.

218
00:36:17,160 --> 00:36:29,060
So when that happens and when we're conquered and when we're sold into slavery and when bad things happen in battle, please allow us to turn back and face you at this temple and pray to you and you restore us when we turn and repent.

219
00:36:29,060 --> 00:36:29,900
Right?

220
00:36:29,900 --> 00:36:31,200
That was the prayer.

221
00:36:33,200 --> 00:36:45,880
And in 2 Chronicles 7, 14, the Lord replies to him, if my people who are called by my name will humble themselves and pray and seek my face and turn from their wicked ways, then I will hear from heaven.

222
00:36:45,880 --> 00:36:46,480
Not the temple.

223
00:36:46,480 --> 00:36:47,400
I'm everywhere.

224
00:36:47,400 --> 00:36:49,780
You can take the wings of the morning, go to the end of the ocean, and I'm there.

225
00:36:49,780 --> 00:36:50,100
Right?

226
00:36:50,100 --> 00:36:53,900
I will hear from heaven and will redeem your land.

227
00:36:53,900 --> 00:36:54,620
I will heal you.

228
00:36:54,620 --> 00:36:55,380
I will have you back.

229
00:36:55,380 --> 00:36:58,020
You will be forgiven if you repent, just like I promised Moses.

230
00:36:58,020 --> 00:36:58,340
Right?

231
00:36:58,340 --> 00:37:00,600
So this is the nation that was built.

232
00:37:01,240 --> 00:37:11,060
And unfortunately, when Solomon passed away, the wealth and power of the kingdom was too enticing to his heirs.

233
00:37:11,060 --> 00:37:12,560
And they split the kingdom in half.

234
00:37:12,560 --> 00:37:14,520
The northern kingdom that kept the name of Israel.

235
00:37:14,520 --> 00:37:17,580
The southern kingdom that kept the temple and called itself Judah.

236
00:37:17,580 --> 00:37:24,700
The king of the northern kingdom, Jeroboam, did not want his people going to the southern kingdom to worship God.

237
00:37:25,060 --> 00:37:28,320
And to be, God had given him this temple so they would be unified around him.

238
00:37:28,320 --> 00:37:32,620
Jeroboam now wants them to be divided and to stay and honor his power.

239
00:37:32,620 --> 00:37:38,800
And so what he did in what would forever and more be known in the Bible as the sin of Jeroboam,

240
00:37:38,800 --> 00:37:48,720
in 2 Kings 14, I'm sorry, 1 Kings 12, he built two golden calves, put one on the northern and southern border,

241
00:37:48,820 --> 00:37:51,580
so he never had to leave his kingdom to worship God the way they decided.

242
00:37:51,580 --> 00:37:55,680
And he said, this, O Israel, is your God who brought you up out of Egypt.

243
00:37:55,680 --> 00:38:04,000
And reinstantiated the idolatry that was set up at Mount Sinai that God had spoken out against.

244
00:38:04,960 --> 00:38:11,540
And so the northern kingdom from then on out was in this syncretistic worship where they claimed to worship Yahweh.

245
00:38:11,540 --> 00:38:15,380
And they had the Old Testament, you know, prophecies that we have today.

246
00:38:15,380 --> 00:38:16,460
And they heard from God.

247
00:38:16,460 --> 00:38:23,260
But they worshipped him in the way that would further their desires for their kingdom, not his creation.

248
00:38:23,260 --> 00:38:30,980
And they came to those golden calves and worshipped them with their own feast festival,

249
00:38:30,980 --> 00:38:34,520
their own feast schedule and their own festivals and their own kind of sacrifice.

250
00:38:35,780 --> 00:38:44,200
About 150 years into that kingdom, his descendant, Jeroboam II, came to power and he brought Israel to its greatest grandeur.

251
00:38:44,200 --> 00:38:46,820
Jeroboam's goal was to make Israel great again.

252
00:38:46,820 --> 00:38:52,200
He wanted to reach back and take the virtues of the kingdom of Solomon and use them synthetically

253
00:38:52,200 --> 00:39:00,240
to expand Israel's self-image and boundaries until they were as rich and as grand as Solomon had ever made them.

254
00:39:00,240 --> 00:39:02,620
Not understanding that God had made them that way.

255
00:39:03,220 --> 00:39:10,560
And he wanted to do that by harnessing the golden calves and the idol worship and keeping people in his kingdom focused on him and his goals.

256
00:39:10,560 --> 00:39:15,780
And he had a prophet that was with him who prophesied that God would, in fact,

257
00:39:15,780 --> 00:39:19,120
aid him in expanding his kingdoms, that he would build the great kingdom.

258
00:39:19,120 --> 00:39:20,880
And that prophet's name was Jonah.

259
00:39:20,880 --> 00:39:29,540
Jonah was Jeroboam's prophet who led his people in the worship of the golden calves,

260
00:39:30,040 --> 00:39:34,340
away from centering their lives around Yahweh and the way they'd been instructed.

261
00:39:34,340 --> 00:39:40,960
As Jeroboam expanded his kingdom for his own glory.

262
00:39:40,960 --> 00:39:41,680
Okay?

263
00:39:42,560 --> 00:39:43,660
That's who Jonah was.

264
00:39:43,660 --> 00:39:46,880
And everybody who read this story back then would have known that.

265
00:39:46,880 --> 00:39:50,440
When we read the opening lines, which we're going to read now.

266
00:39:54,040 --> 00:39:56,640
Now the word of the Lord came to Jonah, son of Amittai.

267
00:39:56,640 --> 00:39:58,700
Everybody instantly knows who that is, right?

268
00:39:58,700 --> 00:40:02,120
That's the secretary of state of the northern kingdom, right?

269
00:40:02,120 --> 00:40:05,460
The Lord of the Lord came to Jonah, son of Amittai, saying,

270
00:40:05,460 --> 00:40:09,240
Arise, go to Nineveh, the great city, and call out against it.

271
00:40:09,240 --> 00:40:11,280
For their evil has come up before me.

272
00:40:11,340 --> 00:40:14,840
And he used that phrase all throughout the Old Testament when someone was murdered,

273
00:40:14,840 --> 00:40:16,660
and just the stench of evil coming up, right?

274
00:40:16,660 --> 00:40:19,440
Has come up before me.

275
00:40:19,440 --> 00:40:24,580
But Jonah rose and fled to Tarshish, away from the presence of the Lord.

276
00:40:24,580 --> 00:40:28,180
And he went down to Joppa and found a ship going to Tarshish.

277
00:40:28,180 --> 00:40:32,640
And he paid the fare and went down into it to go with them to Tarshish,

278
00:40:32,640 --> 00:40:34,120
away from the presence of the Lord.

279
00:40:34,580 --> 00:40:36,860
And the Lord hurled a great wind upon the sea.

280
00:40:36,860 --> 00:40:38,880
And there was a mighty tempest on the sea.

281
00:40:38,880 --> 00:40:40,580
And the ship threatened to break up.

282
00:40:40,580 --> 00:40:42,640
And then the mariners were afraid.

283
00:40:42,640 --> 00:40:44,900
And each one cried out to his own God.

284
00:40:44,900 --> 00:40:49,560
And they hurled the cargo that was in the ship into the sea to lighten it for them.

285
00:40:49,560 --> 00:40:52,600
But Jonah had gone down into the innermost bowels of the ship

286
00:40:52,600 --> 00:40:54,940
and had lain down and was fast asleep.

287
00:40:54,940 --> 00:40:56,340
So the captain came to him and said,

288
00:40:56,340 --> 00:40:57,760
What do you mean, you sleeper?

289
00:40:57,760 --> 00:40:59,400
Arise, call out to your God.

290
00:40:59,400 --> 00:41:02,740
Perhaps the God will give a thought to us so that we may not perish.

291
00:41:03,700 --> 00:41:04,700
And they said to one another,

292
00:41:04,700 --> 00:41:08,520
Come, let's cast lots, which is rolling dice or drawing straws or something.

293
00:41:08,520 --> 00:41:13,220
Cast lots so that we may know on whose account this evil has come upon us.

294
00:41:13,220 --> 00:41:15,340
So they cast lots and the lot fell on Jonah.

295
00:41:15,340 --> 00:41:17,160
And then they said to him,

296
00:41:17,160 --> 00:41:19,980
Tell us on whose account this evil has come upon us.

297
00:41:19,980 --> 00:41:20,780
What's your occupation?

298
00:41:20,780 --> 00:41:22,040
Where do you come from?

299
00:41:22,040 --> 00:41:22,720
What's your country?

300
00:41:22,720 --> 00:41:24,380
Of what people are you?

301
00:41:24,380 --> 00:41:25,400
And he said to them,

302
00:41:25,400 --> 00:41:26,540
I am a Hebrew,

303
00:41:26,540 --> 00:41:29,760
which was a term that the outsiders used speaking of them.

304
00:41:29,760 --> 00:41:30,600
So he's speaking to foreigners.

305
00:41:30,600 --> 00:41:31,740
I am a Hebrew,

306
00:41:32,020 --> 00:41:33,920
and I fear the Lord God of heaven,

307
00:41:33,920 --> 00:41:36,080
who made the sea and the dry land.

308
00:41:36,080 --> 00:41:38,280
And then the men were exceedingly afraid.

309
00:41:38,280 --> 00:41:39,040
And they said to them,

310
00:41:39,040 --> 00:41:40,520
What is this you've done?

311
00:41:40,520 --> 00:41:42,840
For the men knew that he was fleeing from the presence of the Lord,

312
00:41:42,840 --> 00:41:43,920
because he told them.

313
00:41:43,920 --> 00:41:46,060
And then they said to him,

314
00:41:46,060 --> 00:41:49,000
What shall we do to you that the sea may quiet down for us?

315
00:41:49,000 --> 00:41:51,360
For the sea had grown more and more tempestuous.

316
00:41:51,360 --> 00:41:52,460
And he said to them,

317
00:41:52,460 --> 00:41:54,360
Pick me up and hurl me into the sea,

318
00:41:54,620 --> 00:41:56,180
and the sea will quiet down for you.

319
00:41:56,180 --> 00:41:59,420
For I know it's because of me that this great tempest has come upon you.

320
00:41:59,420 --> 00:42:00,000
Nevertheless,

321
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:02,260
the men rode hard to try to get back to land,

322
00:42:02,260 --> 00:42:03,240
but they could not,

323
00:42:03,240 --> 00:42:05,580
for the sea grew more and more tempestuous against them.

324
00:42:05,580 --> 00:42:07,520
And therefore they called out to the Lord,

325
00:42:07,520 --> 00:42:08,040
O Lord,

326
00:42:08,040 --> 00:42:10,280
let us not perish for this man's life,

327
00:42:10,280 --> 00:42:12,580
and lay not on us his innocent blood.

328
00:42:12,580 --> 00:42:13,060
For you,

329
00:42:13,060 --> 00:42:13,340
O Lord,

330
00:42:13,340 --> 00:42:14,360
have done as it pleased you.

331
00:42:15,020 --> 00:42:17,280
So they picked Jonah up and hurled him into the sea,

332
00:42:17,280 --> 00:42:19,100
and the sea ceased from its raging.

333
00:42:19,100 --> 00:42:21,460
And then the men feared the Lord exceedingly.

334
00:42:21,460 --> 00:42:23,720
And they offered a sacrifice to the Lord,

335
00:42:23,720 --> 00:42:24,520
and they made vows.

336
00:42:24,520 --> 00:42:27,840
And the Lord appointed a great fish to swallow up Jonah.

337
00:42:27,840 --> 00:42:31,520
And Jonah was in the belly of the fish for three days and three nights.

338
00:42:31,520 --> 00:42:37,020
That is a well-told tale,

339
00:42:37,020 --> 00:42:40,220
and it is a story about repentance and rebellion.

340
00:42:40,220 --> 00:42:41,500
Repentance,

341
00:42:41,500 --> 00:42:43,600
because maybe more than any other story in the Bible,

342
00:42:44,420 --> 00:42:45,840
this literally,

343
00:42:45,840 --> 00:42:49,680
physically is a picture of someone fleeing from the will of God,

344
00:42:49,680 --> 00:42:52,520
needing to turn and come back to God.

345
00:42:52,520 --> 00:42:55,320
It's a physical picture of repentance and the failure to do it.

346
00:42:55,320 --> 00:42:57,800
It's about rebellion,

347
00:42:57,800 --> 00:42:59,300
because Jonah thinks,

348
00:42:59,300 --> 00:43:00,820
God can't make me do it,

349
00:43:00,820 --> 00:43:01,420
right?

350
00:43:01,420 --> 00:43:03,900
I'll just get in a boat and go the other way.

351
00:43:03,900 --> 00:43:04,920
You can't stop my boat.

352
00:43:04,920 --> 00:43:05,420
And God's like,

353
00:43:05,420 --> 00:43:05,580
well,

354
00:43:05,580 --> 00:43:06,580
God can,

355
00:43:06,580 --> 00:43:06,960
you know.

356
00:43:06,960 --> 00:43:08,160
Are you sure?

357
00:43:08,160 --> 00:43:09,700
And he's like,

358
00:43:09,700 --> 00:43:09,920
well,

359
00:43:09,920 --> 00:43:10,760
then I'll just drown.

360
00:43:10,760 --> 00:43:11,360
And God says,

361
00:43:11,360 --> 00:43:12,120
are you sure?

362
00:43:12,120 --> 00:43:12,660
You know?

363
00:43:13,820 --> 00:43:17,540
And so he believes that he's going to keep rebelling.

364
00:43:17,540 --> 00:43:20,640
And the key thing,

365
00:43:20,640 --> 00:43:22,040
this is so well told,

366
00:43:22,040 --> 00:43:23,560
there's many literary devices.

367
00:43:23,560 --> 00:43:24,780
We're just going to focus on a few.

368
00:43:24,780 --> 00:43:27,680
One is that everything is so deliberately out of place,

369
00:43:27,680 --> 00:43:28,780
the way the author writes it.

370
00:43:29,460 --> 00:43:31,560
We have a prophet of God defying God.

371
00:43:31,560 --> 00:43:32,320
That ain't normal.

372
00:43:32,320 --> 00:43:37,780
We have a man deliberately fleeing into danger because he wants the danger,

373
00:43:37,780 --> 00:43:38,280
right?

374
00:43:38,280 --> 00:43:39,180
That's not normal.

375
00:43:39,180 --> 00:43:42,460
His priorities in life have gotten twisted,

376
00:43:42,460 --> 00:43:42,820
right?

377
00:43:42,820 --> 00:43:44,600
We have him,

378
00:43:44,600 --> 00:43:44,840
yes,

379
00:43:44,840 --> 00:43:45,480
surviving,

380
00:43:45,480 --> 00:43:46,740
being ingested by a fish,

381
00:43:46,740 --> 00:43:47,160
right?

382
00:43:47,160 --> 00:43:47,980
That's not normal.

383
00:43:47,980 --> 00:43:49,680
Everything's just sort of out of place.

384
00:43:50,020 --> 00:43:52,480
And it would have been just as odd to them then as it is now.

385
00:43:52,480 --> 00:43:56,840
Maybe more odd to them because they've got a lot more implications of this without hearing it explained.

386
00:43:56,840 --> 00:44:07,020
And the motif of the sea that we must understand that they would have heard is everyone understood the sea to be the primordial chaos that was out to get them,

387
00:44:07,020 --> 00:44:10,580
out of which God had brought forth the good world when he created it.

388
00:44:10,900 --> 00:44:15,940
Like God had raised the good clean land that they could live on out of the primordial chaos of the sea,

389
00:44:15,940 --> 00:44:19,920
which like in the times of Noah was always ready to come sweep them away,

390
00:44:19,920 --> 00:44:20,380
right?

391
00:44:20,380 --> 00:44:25,120
And so there's always a fear of the sea and of what's down at the bottom,

392
00:44:25,120 --> 00:44:26,340
which is death and shield.

393
00:44:26,340 --> 00:44:27,140
When they say shield,

394
00:44:27,140 --> 00:44:29,180
it's down at the bottom of the primordial chaos.

395
00:44:29,180 --> 00:44:31,360
And there's frequently throughout the Old Testament,

396
00:44:31,360 --> 00:44:37,980
the messenger of God will experience and exhibit the power of God by overcoming the power of the sea,

397
00:44:37,980 --> 00:44:39,180
the primordial chaos,

398
00:44:39,180 --> 00:44:39,480
right?

399
00:44:39,820 --> 00:44:42,260
Think about Moses coming through the Red Sea,

400
00:44:42,260 --> 00:44:42,840
for instance.

401
00:44:42,840 --> 00:44:43,840
There's many examples,

402
00:44:43,840 --> 00:44:45,040
Noah being an obvious one.

403
00:44:45,040 --> 00:44:46,060
And in Revelation,

404
00:44:46,060 --> 00:44:48,020
when it says there'll be a time with no more sea,

405
00:44:48,020 --> 00:44:49,380
the land,

406
00:44:49,380 --> 00:44:52,020
the creation is fully built and there's no more primordial chaos.

407
00:44:52,020 --> 00:44:52,680
That's the image,

408
00:44:52,680 --> 00:44:53,000
right?

409
00:44:53,000 --> 00:44:55,920
But Jonah wants to be chucked into it,

410
00:44:55,920 --> 00:44:56,560
right?

411
00:44:56,560 --> 00:44:59,440
He's trying to get away from God's good creation.

412
00:44:59,440 --> 00:45:03,280
And so we see these rapid movements.

413
00:45:03,280 --> 00:45:08,740
The story's told in a really rapid fire style that exists in Hebrew that allows you to run through a story

414
00:45:08,740 --> 00:45:11,140
till you get to the important part that you want people to focus on.

415
00:45:11,140 --> 00:45:12,360
And in that bit,

416
00:45:12,360 --> 00:45:15,520
it says he goes down to the sea and down into the bottom of the boat,

417
00:45:15,520 --> 00:45:17,420
as close to Sheol as he can,

418
00:45:17,420 --> 00:45:18,800
and then down into the water.

419
00:45:18,800 --> 00:45:21,360
And then the great fish takes him down.

420
00:45:21,360 --> 00:45:25,960
And the image of being in the fish's belly is that he's being dragged down to Sheol,

421
00:45:25,960 --> 00:45:26,340
right?

422
00:45:27,080 --> 00:45:28,660
It's the image of a living death.

423
00:45:28,660 --> 00:45:35,420
And so that rapid fire narrative is rushing us through till we can get to them talking.

424
00:45:35,420 --> 00:45:38,960
And you'll just have to take my word for this.

425
00:45:38,960 --> 00:45:43,440
This first chapter is carefully structured in rings around a central core statement.

426
00:45:44,360 --> 00:45:45,840
And that core statement is,

427
00:45:45,840 --> 00:45:52,640
I serve the Lord God of heaven who made the sea and the dry land.

428
00:45:52,640 --> 00:45:55,920
You would think the central part would be,

429
00:45:55,920 --> 00:45:57,280
I'm running away from God,

430
00:45:57,280 --> 00:46:00,520
but the author has carefully crafted it so that that's sort of said as an after statement.

431
00:46:00,520 --> 00:46:02,580
Because while that's important to the story,

432
00:46:02,580 --> 00:46:04,880
it's not the key thing that's going on in the world,

433
00:46:04,880 --> 00:46:05,260
right?

434
00:46:06,440 --> 00:46:09,920
Jonah fears the Lord God of heaven who made the sea and the dry land.

435
00:46:09,920 --> 00:46:11,520
And when the author says that,

436
00:46:11,520 --> 00:46:13,040
he's having Jonah quote Scripture.

437
00:46:13,040 --> 00:46:14,000
He's quoting,

438
00:46:14,000 --> 00:46:16,020
he's referencing Psalm 95.

439
00:46:16,020 --> 00:46:19,380
For the Lord is great God and great King above all gods.

440
00:46:19,380 --> 00:46:21,000
In his hands are the depths of the earth,

441
00:46:21,000 --> 00:46:22,480
and the heights of the mountains are also his,

442
00:46:22,480 --> 00:46:23,460
and the sea is his,

443
00:46:23,460 --> 00:46:24,180
for he made it,

444
00:46:24,180 --> 00:46:25,580
for he formed the dry land.

445
00:46:25,580 --> 00:46:26,140
Today,

446
00:46:26,140 --> 00:46:26,920
if you hear my voice,

447
00:46:26,920 --> 00:46:29,360
don't harden your hearts as you did in the wilderness.

448
00:46:29,360 --> 00:46:33,200
This is referencing a psalm that Jonah knows,

449
00:46:33,200 --> 00:46:36,320
which is referencing what happened just after,

450
00:46:36,440 --> 00:46:39,700
when they're out in the desert and they harden their hearts and wouldn't follow God,

451
00:46:39,700 --> 00:46:40,340
right?

452
00:46:40,340 --> 00:46:44,520
He is the God who's with you everywhere of the sea and the dry land.

453
00:46:44,520 --> 00:46:45,440
And Jonah's saying,

454
00:46:45,440 --> 00:46:46,800
I know he's in control.

455
00:46:46,800 --> 00:46:50,220
I know I shouldn't harden my heart and rebel against him.

456
00:46:50,220 --> 00:46:50,780
But oh,

457
00:46:50,780 --> 00:46:51,220
by the way,

458
00:46:51,220 --> 00:46:52,820
I'm fleeing from his face,

459
00:46:52,820 --> 00:46:53,340
right?

460
00:46:53,340 --> 00:46:57,860
Everything in this story is obeying God.

461
00:46:57,860 --> 00:47:00,000
The sea does what he wants.

462
00:47:00,000 --> 00:47:00,740
The winds,

463
00:47:00,740 --> 00:47:02,400
he hurls the winds.

464
00:47:02,400 --> 00:47:03,280
The boat,

465
00:47:03,280 --> 00:47:05,100
the language in Hebrew,

466
00:47:05,100 --> 00:47:07,020
the boat has almost thoughts.

467
00:47:07,020 --> 00:47:08,320
It's thinking about breaking up,

468
00:47:08,320 --> 00:47:10,060
but it's holding it together because he's supposed to,

469
00:47:10,060 --> 00:47:10,380
right?

470
00:47:10,380 --> 00:47:12,020
Everybody's obeying God,

471
00:47:12,020 --> 00:47:14,340
including the pagan fishermen,

472
00:47:14,340 --> 00:47:14,960
right?

473
00:47:14,960 --> 00:47:17,960
Who fear the Lord and are trying really hard not to kill this guy,

474
00:47:17,960 --> 00:47:18,400
right?

475
00:47:18,400 --> 00:47:21,560
Except for Jonah.

476
00:47:21,560 --> 00:47:26,900
And we should notice that the prophet comes through the waters as a hero of the Old Testament

477
00:47:26,900 --> 00:47:27,320
should,

478
00:47:27,320 --> 00:47:29,700
and is willing to give his life and intercession for others.

479
00:47:29,700 --> 00:47:32,080
But he's doing it selfishly this time.

480
00:47:32,160 --> 00:47:33,700
He's not actually caring about them.

481
00:47:33,700 --> 00:47:36,220
God has told him,

482
00:47:36,220 --> 00:47:38,540
go to the great city whose evil has come up before me,

483
00:47:38,540 --> 00:47:42,120
referencing all the murder of the Old Testament when he says that phrase.

484
00:47:42,120 --> 00:47:45,760
And Jonah could go out and try to save those people,

485
00:47:45,760 --> 00:47:46,860
but he is not interested.

486
00:47:46,860 --> 00:47:50,240
He's not here to save anybody who's not from Israel,

487
00:47:50,240 --> 00:47:50,960
right?

488
00:47:53,120 --> 00:47:59,920
He should have been prophesying to repentance to his nation who was living in pagan idolatry.

489
00:47:59,920 --> 00:48:05,420
And God has sent him to see another nation who is also living in pagan idolatry,

490
00:48:05,420 --> 00:48:07,520
surely hoping he'll notice the similarity,

491
00:48:07,520 --> 00:48:09,640
and that when they repent,

492
00:48:09,640 --> 00:48:11,880
this will be a message that he can bring back to his people.

493
00:48:11,880 --> 00:48:13,620
But Jonah wants no part of it.

494
00:48:13,620 --> 00:48:15,020
He's sent to Nineveh.

495
00:48:15,020 --> 00:48:15,660
Incidentally,

496
00:48:15,660 --> 00:48:17,600
Nineveh is modern-day Mosul in Iraq.

497
00:48:18,900 --> 00:48:22,160
So throw the map up there for a minute.

498
00:48:22,160 --> 00:48:25,700
So it would become, though,

499
00:48:25,700 --> 00:48:26,540
the capital of Assyria.

500
00:48:26,540 --> 00:48:27,500
God sends him,

501
00:48:27,500 --> 00:48:31,900
asks him to go 750 miles northeast to what's now Mosul.

502
00:48:31,900 --> 00:48:34,360
Instead, he gets on a boat.

503
00:48:34,360 --> 00:48:35,640
He goes down, down, down.

504
00:48:35,640 --> 00:48:39,600
Gets on a boat and travels 3,000 miles west.

505
00:48:39,600 --> 00:48:41,860
Or tries to.

506
00:48:42,180 --> 00:48:43,720
He goes through the Straits of Gibraltar,

507
00:48:43,720 --> 00:48:44,440
out into the Atlantic,

508
00:48:44,440 --> 00:48:45,640
to the far side of Spain,

509
00:48:45,640 --> 00:48:46,260
right?

510
00:48:46,260 --> 00:48:47,920
That's where he wants to get to.

511
00:48:47,920 --> 00:48:50,400
As far as he knows,

512
00:48:50,400 --> 00:48:52,280
that's where all the maps of the world end.

513
00:48:52,280 --> 00:48:54,420
He's literally going out into the primordial chaos.

514
00:48:54,420 --> 00:48:58,940
And he's doing this to get away from the major city of Nineveh.

515
00:48:58,940 --> 00:48:59,900
It's not the capital yet.

516
00:48:59,900 --> 00:49:00,680
It will become the capital.

517
00:49:00,680 --> 00:49:03,640
But it's like the New York City of Assyria.

518
00:49:03,640 --> 00:49:06,600
And it's populated by a violent, brutal enemy.

519
00:49:06,600 --> 00:49:10,360
Nahum later will describe some of their acts,

520
00:49:10,380 --> 00:49:11,080
if you want to go read it,

521
00:49:11,080 --> 00:49:12,660
or if you just want to even look at their art.

522
00:49:12,660 --> 00:49:14,580
The artwork of the Assyrians

523
00:49:14,580 --> 00:49:15,800
shows, you know,

524
00:49:15,800 --> 00:49:18,300
mutilated and flayed enemies

525
00:49:18,300 --> 00:49:19,500
because what they wanted to do

526
00:49:19,500 --> 00:49:21,920
was spread terror around to anybody who wasn't them.

527
00:49:21,920 --> 00:49:25,040
They were what Jeroboam was trying to make Israel.

528
00:49:25,040 --> 00:49:28,920
God was trying to make them repent

529
00:49:28,920 --> 00:49:31,060
as he had told Israel to do.

530
00:49:31,060 --> 00:49:34,160
They were all fleeing the wrong direction.

531
00:49:34,160 --> 00:49:34,920
And Jonah, therefore,

532
00:49:34,920 --> 00:49:36,760
had very real reason to hate and fear them.

533
00:49:36,760 --> 00:49:38,520
These two violent cultures

534
00:49:38,520 --> 00:49:39,820
that were trying to be the dominant,

535
00:49:40,300 --> 00:49:43,740
country in this region.

536
00:49:43,740 --> 00:49:44,960
They couldn't both exist.

537
00:49:44,960 --> 00:49:46,320
They knew there was a reckoning coming.

538
00:49:46,320 --> 00:49:51,980
And it was the undoing of Jonah's life work

539
00:49:51,980 --> 00:49:53,180
to try to save these people.

540
00:49:53,180 --> 00:49:58,740
God points out that there's great evil

541
00:49:58,740 --> 00:50:00,280
and he wants to restore his creation.

542
00:50:00,280 --> 00:50:01,280
He wants to correct it.

543
00:50:01,280 --> 00:50:03,780
Jonah wants only to restore his nation.

544
00:50:05,280 --> 00:50:07,360
Plus, it would have been very unsettling

545
00:50:07,360 --> 00:50:08,360
both to Jeroboam

546
00:50:08,360 --> 00:50:11,360
and to Shalmaneser, capital of Assyria,

547
00:50:11,360 --> 00:50:12,440
if, you know,

548
00:50:12,440 --> 00:50:14,340
Jonah had walked into his country

549
00:50:14,340 --> 00:50:15,960
and started telling them how to behave

550
00:50:15,960 --> 00:50:16,880
to obey his God

551
00:50:16,880 --> 00:50:18,040
so that they would be saved.

552
00:50:18,040 --> 00:50:19,320
I don't think either king

553
00:50:19,320 --> 00:50:20,940
would be excited about that, right?

554
00:50:20,940 --> 00:50:23,020
It would have been dangerous for Jonah.

555
00:50:23,020 --> 00:50:24,860
It would have been the end of his career, right?

556
00:50:24,860 --> 00:50:26,880
It's a very difficult thing

557
00:50:26,880 --> 00:50:27,800
he's being asked to do

558
00:50:27,800 --> 00:50:29,400
independent of all the rest of this story.

559
00:50:31,180 --> 00:50:32,220
And so we see in the end

560
00:50:32,220 --> 00:50:34,080
that Jonah basically believed three things.

561
00:50:34,080 --> 00:50:36,520
He believed in the absolute sovereignty of God.

562
00:50:36,520 --> 00:50:38,660
He believed that repentance would occur

563
00:50:38,660 --> 00:50:39,960
if God sent him on this mission

564
00:50:39,960 --> 00:50:42,240
and that God would forgive these people.

565
00:50:42,240 --> 00:50:43,180
He says that clearly.

566
00:50:43,180 --> 00:50:46,820
He also believes in the conservation of mass.

567
00:50:46,820 --> 00:50:48,360
He believes if he's not in Assyria,

568
00:50:48,360 --> 00:50:51,240
if he's on his way to Tarshish,

569
00:50:51,240 --> 00:50:52,040
he can't be in Assyria

570
00:50:52,040 --> 00:50:53,540
and therefore he can't help, right?

571
00:50:53,540 --> 00:50:55,780
He thinks he can foil the plan

572
00:50:55,780 --> 00:50:56,540
by not being there.

573
00:50:56,540 --> 00:50:58,060
And the third thing he believes

574
00:50:58,060 --> 00:50:59,780
is that he is not gonna help Assyria.

575
00:51:00,340 --> 00:51:01,380
He's just not gonna do it

576
00:51:01,380 --> 00:51:04,620
because he knows exactly who God is.

577
00:51:04,620 --> 00:51:06,860
If we're not sure about it in chapter one,

578
00:51:06,860 --> 00:51:08,880
by chapter four, he says to God,

579
00:51:08,880 --> 00:51:11,300
I knew that you're a gracious and compassionate God,

580
00:51:11,300 --> 00:51:12,860
slow to anger, abundant in mercy,

581
00:51:12,860 --> 00:51:14,580
one who relents from disaster.

582
00:51:14,580 --> 00:51:17,960
He's quoting God from Exodus 34

583
00:51:17,960 --> 00:51:19,060
where he says who he is.

584
00:51:19,060 --> 00:51:21,280
He understands his place in his story.

585
00:51:21,280 --> 00:51:23,200
And he says, I knew you were that God

586
00:51:23,200 --> 00:51:24,920
and because I've joined in your effort

587
00:51:24,920 --> 00:51:26,300
to save the Ninevites,

588
00:51:26,300 --> 00:51:27,400
it's better that I should die

589
00:51:27,400 --> 00:51:29,160
than that I should live, right?

590
00:51:29,500 --> 00:51:33,340
So he ran saying, if I'm absent, I can't help.

591
00:51:33,340 --> 00:51:35,240
If I die, I'll remain absent

592
00:51:35,240 --> 00:51:38,100
because he never doubted that if they repented,

593
00:51:38,100 --> 00:51:39,180
God would forgive them.

594
00:51:39,180 --> 00:51:42,260
And mostly then this is about his refusal to prophesy.

595
00:51:42,260 --> 00:51:44,160
And incidentally, when he does prophesy,

596
00:51:44,160 --> 00:51:47,780
he famously says as few words as possible.

597
00:51:47,780 --> 00:51:49,080
He just runs into the city and says,

598
00:51:49,080 --> 00:51:49,940
you're all gonna die.

599
00:51:50,260 --> 00:51:52,800
You know, it's, he only says five words.

600
00:51:52,800 --> 00:51:54,620
He's the worst prophet on record.

601
00:51:54,620 --> 00:51:57,060
And God still uses it.

602
00:51:57,060 --> 00:51:58,920
Nineveh still repents.

603
00:51:58,920 --> 00:52:00,360
Just like in chapter one,

604
00:52:00,360 --> 00:52:02,280
he says to the men on the ship,

605
00:52:02,280 --> 00:52:04,820
yep, you know, I'm running from God.

606
00:52:04,820 --> 00:52:05,840
He controls the world.

607
00:52:05,840 --> 00:52:07,240
I guess you're gonna have to murder me.

608
00:52:07,240 --> 00:52:10,020
You know, and somehow that saves them.

609
00:52:10,020 --> 00:52:11,160
They turn to God.

610
00:52:11,160 --> 00:52:13,620
They make vows and build a sacrifice

611
00:52:13,620 --> 00:52:14,860
and come to fear God

612
00:52:14,860 --> 00:52:16,380
because God can show his power

613
00:52:16,380 --> 00:52:18,800
independent of how badly we do our jobs, okay?

614
00:52:18,800 --> 00:52:21,200
Not that you should try to do your job badly.

615
00:52:21,200 --> 00:52:24,560
The moral of the story is not to copy Jonah,

616
00:52:24,560 --> 00:52:29,380
but one thing, one question remains then

617
00:52:29,380 --> 00:52:30,880
is did Jonah ever repent?

618
00:52:30,880 --> 00:52:32,240
Was he like too far gone?

619
00:52:32,240 --> 00:52:35,760
And did Nineveh ever fully repent?

620
00:52:35,760 --> 00:52:36,580
It seems they did.

621
00:52:36,580 --> 00:52:38,500
They went back on it in a few generations.

622
00:52:38,500 --> 00:52:40,020
And if you wanna read Nahum

623
00:52:40,020 --> 00:52:42,140
and see what happened next, you may.

624
00:52:44,240 --> 00:52:46,300
But we have to realize that at least in part,

625
00:52:46,300 --> 00:52:48,180
this story had to have been self-reported.

626
00:52:48,180 --> 00:52:50,160
Nobody but Jonah and God

627
00:52:50,160 --> 00:52:52,440
were there in chapter two and chapter four, right?

628
00:52:52,440 --> 00:52:54,520
So the only way we can know about that

629
00:52:54,520 --> 00:52:55,540
is if Jonah told somebody.

630
00:52:55,540 --> 00:52:57,480
So that gives us hope.

631
00:52:57,480 --> 00:52:58,700
As Tim Keller actually said,

632
00:52:58,700 --> 00:52:59,680
Jonah must have realized

633
00:52:59,680 --> 00:53:01,160
he was as wicked as the Ninevites

634
00:53:01,160 --> 00:53:04,400
and that he too was living only by the mercy of God.

635
00:53:04,400 --> 00:53:06,940
And so this book is Jonah's confession

636
00:53:06,940 --> 00:53:08,000
of his shortcomings,

637
00:53:08,000 --> 00:53:10,980
which gives us hope that he may have gone back

638
00:53:10,980 --> 00:53:12,740
and tried to persuade Israel to repent.

639
00:53:13,620 --> 00:53:16,380
But for what we know in chapter one,

640
00:53:16,380 --> 00:53:18,040
he runs down, down, down,

641
00:53:18,040 --> 00:53:19,800
away from the presence of God

642
00:53:19,800 --> 00:53:21,740
into the chaos of the sea.

643
00:53:21,740 --> 00:53:24,140
As we saw on the map,

644
00:53:24,140 --> 00:53:25,960
3,000 miles the other way to Tarshish.

645
00:53:25,960 --> 00:53:26,760
And Tarshish, by the way,

646
00:53:26,760 --> 00:53:27,860
is three things in this story.

647
00:53:27,860 --> 00:53:28,720
The people that are reading it

648
00:53:28,720 --> 00:53:29,680
would have immediately recognized.

649
00:53:29,680 --> 00:53:31,360
One, it's not Nineveh, right?

650
00:53:31,360 --> 00:53:32,860
He's trying to stay away from Nineveh.

651
00:53:32,860 --> 00:53:35,100
Number two, it's the ends of the earth

652
00:53:35,100 --> 00:53:36,360
like we saw on the map, right?

653
00:53:36,360 --> 00:53:38,820
It's as far off the map as you can get.

654
00:53:38,820 --> 00:53:40,260
Nothing past that is charted.

655
00:53:40,900 --> 00:53:41,780
And third thing,

656
00:53:41,780 --> 00:53:43,720
it's the place where Solomon had gone

657
00:53:43,720 --> 00:53:46,080
to get all his riches and jewels

658
00:53:46,080 --> 00:53:47,920
and the things that he used to build his kingdom,

659
00:53:47,920 --> 00:53:48,820
to make it grating.

660
00:53:48,820 --> 00:53:50,780
And Jonah's still in his mind.

661
00:53:50,780 --> 00:53:52,480
I mean, we didn't go down on a royal barge.

662
00:53:52,480 --> 00:53:53,580
Jeroboam didn't send him.

663
00:53:53,580 --> 00:53:55,860
He bought a ticket on a passing fisherman ship.

664
00:53:56,080 --> 00:53:57,340
But he's going to the place

665
00:53:57,340 --> 00:53:58,940
where he might be able to find the strength

666
00:53:58,940 --> 00:54:00,780
to build the kingdom on his own, right?

667
00:54:00,780 --> 00:54:05,760
He thinks he can control his location on the planet.

668
00:54:05,760 --> 00:54:07,420
But in doing that,

669
00:54:07,420 --> 00:54:09,240
he's assuming he can harness God's creation

670
00:54:09,240 --> 00:54:10,240
to thwart God's will,

671
00:54:10,240 --> 00:54:11,460
which is a serious mistake.

672
00:54:11,460 --> 00:54:14,260
God hurls the wind at him and blows him back.

673
00:54:14,260 --> 00:54:16,420
The pagan sailors, on the other hand,

674
00:54:16,420 --> 00:54:18,880
they fear God more than the prophet does.

675
00:54:18,880 --> 00:54:20,200
And they begin to,

676
00:54:20,200 --> 00:54:21,500
they're so upset by the storm,

677
00:54:21,580 --> 00:54:24,500
they're panicking and they're acting in unsailerly ways.

678
00:54:24,500 --> 00:54:25,500
They throw their life,

679
00:54:25,500 --> 00:54:27,920
you know, all their money that's invested in his voyage,

680
00:54:27,920 --> 00:54:28,700
they throw it overboard.

681
00:54:28,700 --> 00:54:30,340
So they're destitute

682
00:54:30,340 --> 00:54:32,220
and probably going to be sold to slaves when they get back.

683
00:54:32,220 --> 00:54:33,240
That's how bad the storm is.

684
00:54:33,240 --> 00:54:34,540
They're willing to risk that, right?

685
00:54:34,540 --> 00:54:37,140
They're trying to row in the storm

686
00:54:37,140 --> 00:54:38,700
to save Jonah's life, which is absurd.

687
00:54:38,700 --> 00:54:39,600
You can't row in a storm.

688
00:54:39,600 --> 00:54:40,920
You're going to lose or break your oars.

689
00:54:40,920 --> 00:54:41,960
Everyone knows it's not,

690
00:54:41,960 --> 00:54:44,280
but they're just trying everything they can

691
00:54:44,280 --> 00:54:49,380
because they're resisting shedding innocent blood.

692
00:54:51,580 --> 00:54:53,300
This will be echoed later in the New Testament

693
00:54:53,300 --> 00:54:55,100
as Pilate, another Gentile,

694
00:54:55,100 --> 00:54:58,660
sent to put on a collision course

695
00:54:58,660 --> 00:55:00,720
with a messenger of God who's about to be killed,

696
00:55:00,720 --> 00:55:02,920
washes his hands and said,

697
00:55:02,920 --> 00:55:04,960
the blood of this innocent man is not on me.

698
00:55:04,960 --> 00:55:09,440
And in the bottom of the boat,

699
00:55:09,440 --> 00:55:11,140
while all this chaos is going on,

700
00:55:11,140 --> 00:55:12,580
Jonah is asleep.

701
00:55:12,580 --> 00:55:15,160
Why on earth is that?

702
00:55:15,160 --> 00:55:16,080
And more importantly,

703
00:55:16,080 --> 00:55:18,320
where else in the Bible do we see another person

704
00:55:18,320 --> 00:55:19,600
sleeping in a boat in the storm?

705
00:55:20,840 --> 00:55:23,000
Ah, Jesus, right, Mark 4, right?

706
00:55:23,000 --> 00:55:26,040
Again, we see both Jonah and Jesus

707
00:55:26,040 --> 00:55:27,960
in a boat with professional fishermen

708
00:55:27,960 --> 00:55:29,040
who are out of their depths

709
00:55:29,040 --> 00:55:30,460
and afraid they're going to die

710
00:55:30,460 --> 00:55:31,960
and their skills are up to no avail

711
00:55:31,960 --> 00:55:34,560
in the great power of the primordial chaos

712
00:55:34,560 --> 00:55:36,320
and the storm that God is in control of,

713
00:55:36,320 --> 00:55:37,180
but they don't know that

714
00:55:37,180 --> 00:55:38,940
or they're forgetting their panic.

715
00:55:38,940 --> 00:55:42,180
And in both cases,

716
00:55:42,180 --> 00:55:44,140
Jonah and Jesus can sleep through it

717
00:55:44,140 --> 00:55:45,820
because they have an absolute assurance

718
00:55:45,820 --> 00:55:48,180
in God's mission to provide salvation

719
00:55:48,180 --> 00:55:50,080
to all nations and their place in it.

720
00:55:50,480 --> 00:55:51,900
They have zero fear of death.

721
00:55:51,900 --> 00:55:54,060
Jesus, because he knows his time has not yet come.

722
00:55:54,060 --> 00:55:57,860
Jonah, because he's kind of hoping it has, right?

723
00:55:57,860 --> 00:56:00,820
He just wants out.

724
00:56:00,820 --> 00:56:04,180
Jesus gets up and calms the storm

725
00:56:04,180 --> 00:56:06,040
out of compassion for his shipmates.

726
00:56:06,040 --> 00:56:09,180
And the sailors in both situations

727
00:56:09,180 --> 00:56:12,560
are just suddenly in awe of the power

728
00:56:12,560 --> 00:56:13,520
that can stop a storm.

729
00:56:14,240 --> 00:56:16,520
And it is a conversion moment for both of them.

730
00:56:16,520 --> 00:56:19,740
They turn and begin the path of repentance

731
00:56:19,740 --> 00:56:21,240
to face back toward God's face

732
00:56:21,240 --> 00:56:22,280
and seek his face

733
00:56:22,280 --> 00:56:24,680
and turn from their pagan ways.

734
00:56:24,680 --> 00:56:27,120
Jonah, at some point,

735
00:56:27,120 --> 00:56:28,620
could have jumped out of the boat himself.

736
00:56:28,620 --> 00:56:29,760
I don't know if you've thought about that.

737
00:56:29,760 --> 00:56:30,460
He could have said,

738
00:56:30,460 --> 00:56:31,440
I'm going to save these guys.

739
